Section 42: Coming into force
(1) The following provisions come into force on the day on which this Act receives Royal Assent—
(1)(a) Part 1 (section 1);
(1)(b) in Part 3—
(1)(b)(i) section 10, but that section has effect in accordance with section 10(4);
(1)(b)(ii) section 11, but that section has effect in accordance with section 11(2);
(1)(b)(iii) section 27, but subsections (2)(d), (3) and (4) of that section have effect in accordance with section 27(5);
(1)(c) Part 4 (sections 29 to 35 and Schedule 3), but it only has effect for the purposes of a Senedd election at which the poll is held on or after 5 April 2021;
(1)(d) in Part 5, sections 37 and 38;
(1)(e) this Part (sections 39 to 43).
(2) Part 2 (sections 2 to 9 and Schedule 1) comes into force on 6 May 2020.
(3) In Part 3—
(3)(a) sections 12 to 26 come into force on 1 June 2020;
(3)(b) section 28 and Schedule 2 come into force on a day appointed by the Welsh Ministers in an order made by statutory instrument.
(4) An order under subsection (3)(b) may include transitional, transitory or saving provision.
(5) In Part 5, section 36 comes into force on the day of the first Senedd election at which the poll is held on or after 5 April 2021.

Section 25: Socially responsible procurement duty: major construction contracts
(1) The particular actions mentioned in section 24(5)(b) are—
(1)(a) having regard to model social public works clauses published by the Welsh Ministers under section 27;
(1)(b) in designing and carrying out procedures preceding the award of the major construction contract, considering whether the contract should include social public works clauses;
(1)(c) in negotiating and awarding the contract, taking all reasonable steps—
(1)(c)(i) to include any social public works clauses it considers should be included;
(1)(c)(ii) to ensure that clauses included in the contract can be implemented;
(1)(d) in managing the contract, taking all reasonable steps to ensure that any social public works clauses included in the contract are implemented;
(1)(e) (see sections 27 to 31 for further provision about the meaning of “social public works clauses” and their application to major construction contracts).
(2) In this Part, a “major construction contract” is a public contract with an estimated value equal to or greater than £2,000,000, which is—
(2)(a) a public works contract,
(2)(b) a works contract, or
(2)(c) a works concession contract.
(3) The Welsh Ministers may by regulations amend this section to modify the meaning of a major construction contract.

Section 39: Annual socially responsible procurement reports
(1) A contracting authority that has awarded any prescribed contracts during a financial year must prepare and publish an annual report on its public procurement as soon as reasonably practicable after the end of that year.
(2) The report must include—
(2)(a) a summary of the public procurement exercises during the year that led to the award of a prescribed contract or were intended to lead to the award of such a contract;
(2)(b) a review of the extent to which all reasonable steps were taken in those public procurement exercises to meet the authority’s socially responsible procurement objectives;
(2)(c) in so far as the authority considers that further steps could reasonably be taken in future public procurement exercises to meet its socially responsible procurement objectives, a statement of how it intends to take those steps;
(2)(d) a summary of the public procurement the authority expects to carry out in the next two financial years;
(2)(e) information of the kind mentioned in subsection (3) that must be specified by regulations made by the Welsh Ministers;
(2)(f) other information as may be specified by regulations made by the Welsh Ministers.
(3) The information referred to in subsection (2)(e) is information that the Welsh Ministers consider they require in order to assess the extent to which—
(3)(a) a contracting authority meeting its socially responsible procurement objectives contributes to the achievement of the well-being goals;
(3)(b) a contracting authority’s public procurement, generally, contributes to the achievement of the well-being goals, for example by—
(3)(b)(i) benefitting the economy of its area, including through the award of contracts to small and medium sized enterprises;
(3)(b)(ii) taking environmental considerations into account;
(3)(b)(iii) taking (other) social considerations into account;
(3)(b)(iv) promoting and facilitating use of the Welsh language.

Section 40: Contracts register
(1) A contracting authority must create, maintain and publish a contracts register.
(2) A contracts register is a register of public contracts entered into by the contracting authority that are of a description prescribed by the Welsh Ministers by regulations (“registrable contracts”).
(3) In relation to each registrable contract, a contracts register must contain—
(3)(a) the date of award of the contract;
(3)(b) the name of the contractor;
(3)(c) the address of the contractor’s principal place of business;
(3)(d) the subject matter;
(3)(e) the estimated value;
(3)(f) the start date;
(3)(g) the end date provided for in the contract (disregarding any option to extend the contract) or, where there is no date specified, a description of the circumstances in which the contract will end;
(3)(h) the duration of any period for which the contract can be extended;
(3)(h)(i) such other information as prescribed by the Welsh Ministers by regulations.
(4) But an authority may withhold from publication an entry, or part of an entry, in the register if it considers that publishing it would—
(4)(a) impede enforcement of the law or otherwise be contrary to the public interest,
(4)(b) prejudice the commercial interests of any person,
(4)(c) prejudice fair competition between economic operators, or
(4)(d) disclose a residential address (in the case of information referred to in subsection (3)(c)).
(5) An authority may delete an entry in its contracts register only after the contract to which it relates has expired or been terminated.

